ightenment? How did ideas penetrate politics and society two centuries ago? How does public opinion influence events? To address these big questions in the history of the modern world, the distinguished historian Robert Darnton poses a comparatively small one: What did the French read in the eighteenth century? The answer lies only partially in the canon of the great Enlightenment philosophes: Voltaire, Diderot, Montesquieu, Rousseau. More popular than these works - indeed, the best-sellers of their time - were other books, also banned by the regime, written and sold \"under the cloak.\" These formed a libertine literature that undercut all the orthodox values of the Old Regime.","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.21,"accessibilityScore":0.214,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"essayistic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"criticism\"","criticism award category"]},{"id":"narrative","score":0.36,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"quest\""]},{"id":"biographical","score":0.34,"confidence":"low","evidence":["biography subject or topic"]},{"id":"popular","score":0.264,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\"","major-award recognition"]},{"id":"accessible","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"France","confidence":0.9}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Popular banned libertine books undercut the orthodox values of the Old Regime and helped connect Enlightenment thought with revolutionary politics and society.","confidence":0.85},"academicOrientation":{"score":42,"confidence":0.88},"profileConfidence":0.9,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"d5a540a17756c176ef1e030feeaa54b86ad6aaff72d6ad0b1901757a7a53297f","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","vali